"In Lesson 9 you saw that a program is a list of exact instructions. But before anyone can write the instructions, they need a method. A clear, step-by-step method that reaches the answer in a limited number of steps is called an algorithm. You already know some. The long division you learned at school is an algorithm.": "f56fbd8f2e94",
"In Lesson 9 you saw that a program is a list of exact instructions. But before anyone can write the instructions, they need a method. A clear, step-by-step method that reaches the answer in a limited number of steps is called an algorithm. You already know some. The long division you learned at school is an algorithm. Here is a problem. A row of closed boxes each holds a number, and you want to find the box that holds 48. One algorithm is very simple: open the first box, then the second, then the third, and go on until you find it. This always works. But with 16 boxes you may have to open all 16. Now suppose the numbers are in order, from small to large. Then there is another algorithm. Open the box in the middle. If its number is too small, the one you want must be to the right, so you can forget the whole left half without opening it. Do the same again with the boxes that are left. With 16 boxes, you never need to open more than 5. The real surprise comes when the boxes increase. Double them, and the first method may need twice as many tries. The second method needs only one more. With a million boxes, opening them one by one could take a million tries. Halving never takes more than 20. There is a catch. Halving works only when the boxes are in order. Mix them up, and it throws away the half where your number is hiding. That is why a dictionary is kept in alphabetical order. Putting things in order is called sorting, and it takes work too. Also, the simple method sometimes wins by luck, when the number happens to be in the first box. So we compare methods by the most steps they could ever need. This is why programmers care so much about algorithms. A computer that is twice as fast can only cut the time in half. A better method can turn a million steps into twenty. The method comes first. Then a program is written to tell the computer how to carry it out.": "f65ce97e3763",
